2026-07-25 -- Reports rebuilt from a form into a screen
================================================================================

First, cleanup: the previous run finished its work but died before committing
and left a stale .git/index.lock behind, so its whole vehicles-board change
was sitting uncommitted. Committed it as-is (6c64085) before starting.
Worth knowing next run, because it will happen again: when this repo is
reached through the sandbox mount, unlink() is refused outright. Every git
write therefore leaves its own .git/*.lock behind and blocks the *next* git
command -- that's what .git/stale-tmp is, a previous run's graveyard. Rename
within the same directory does work, so the fix is to sweep the locks aside
before each git command:

    for f in .git/index.lock .git/HEAD.lock .git/refs/heads/master.lock; do
      [ -e "$f" ] && mv "$f" ".git/stale-tmp/$(basename $f).$RANDOM"
    done

(GIT_INDEX_FILE pointed at /tmp gets `git add` through but not the commit,
since that still needs HEAD.lock.)

Reports was the weakest screen in the app: a form in a card. You landed on it,
it showed you nothing, and you had to fill in two dates and press Generate to
get a bare table with a bold Total row. Everything else in the app had already
moved on -- empty states, skeletons, an error boundary, sortable columns,
remembered preferences -- and Reports had none of it.

It's now a screen:

  * Loads itself. VIEW_LOADERS/VIEW_PLACEHOLDERS entries, so opening it
    fetches, with a skeleton shaped like the cards/chart/table it's about to
    show, and a failure paints the error boundary instead of a toast. Generate
    is gone -- every control refetches on change, so there's nothing to press
    and nothing that can go stale relative to the controls above it.

  * Four summary cards. Vehicles (split recon/we-owe), total cost, average per
    vehicle, and an over-quote count with the dollar amount past estimate --
    flagged warn, and the card's top edge picks up the warning color via
    :has(). They describe exactly the rows below them, same range same
    segment, which is the thing the vehicles board still gets wrong (its stats
    are lot-wide while its table is filtered).

  * A horizontal bar chart. Top 12 by cost, with a hairline marker showing
    where the quote was, bars turning crit when a car ran >10% past it (same
    rule the board colors Cost with). Technicians get labor cost by tech.
    Built from plain elements -- no CDN, inherits the theme, reflows with the
    panel. Bars and markers share one scale that accounts for quotes larger
    than any bar, so an under-budget car's estimate can't pin to the end of
    the track and read as the biggest number on the chart.

  * Sortable columns, blanks-last, keyed per report shape (the two shapes
    share only "cost", so switching reports resets a sort key the new shape
    doesn't have rather than silently sorting by nothing). Print follows the
    sort, so paper matches screen.

  * Download CSV, server-side: /api/export/report/vehicle-spend.csv and
    .../technicians.csv, taking the same start/end/segment the report does
    and built from the same row builders, so the file can't disagree with
    the screen. The link's href tracks the toolbar.

  * Segmented control instead of a dropdown for the four reports, always-on
    range chips, and hand-typed dates that re-light the matching chip (or
    clear all of them, rather than leaving one lit claiming a range that
    isn't in effect). Type/range/sort persisted to localStorage and restored
    before the first fetch.

Two bugs found by actually running it rather than by reading it:
  * A failed refetch left the card and chart skeletons shimmering above the
    error message, because those aren't VIEW_PLACEHOLDERS targets and the
    error boundary never reached them. Found by pointing jsdom at a live
    seeded server and watching the screen when the server went away.
  * With real seed data every car is at $0 (parts ordered, none received) and
    the chart panel simply vanished, which reads like it broke. It now says
    there's nothing to plot yet.

Tests: 176 -> 183.
  * tests/dom/reports_smoke.mjs -- 50-odd assertions with every summary
    number checkable by hand (total 3000 / 4 vehicles / avg 750 / one car
    over by 550), chart scaling including the marker-past-the-max case,
    sorting in both directions with blanks, shape switching, range chips vs.
    hand-typed dates, the CSV href, prefs round-trip, and the empty /
    nothing-to-plot / failed / recovered states. Mutation-tested: dropping
    the marker from the chart scale, dropping the sort reset, dropping the
    prefs save, sorting blanks first, letting the CSV href go stale, and
    rendering unsorted rows each fail it. Two of those only started failing
    after the fixture was fixed to actually exercise them.
  * test_export.py -- both CSV endpoints, including that segment and date
    range reach the file and that an unknown segment is a 422.
  * test_static_assets.py -- report toolbar vs. REPORT_TITLES, range chips
    vs. the ranges the app can detect, every data-report-sort has a spec,
    both shapes keep a cost fallback, no Generate button. All mutation-tested.

Suite green (183; the 3 jsdom cases run via node), server boots, and the
screen was rendered against a real seeded database in both report modes.

Note for next run: this sandbox can't run `uv` (no network to the Python
build host) and the repo's tests/dom/node_modules hangs on read here. Use a
local venv + a /tmp jsdom install; there's a mirror script pattern in this
session's history.

Next time, in rough priority order:
  1. The vehicles board's stat cards still summarize the whole board while
     the table below them is filtered -- Reports now does this correctly and
     the two screens visibly disagree. Scope them to the filter.
  2. Board columns for days-since-last-activity (updated_at is in the
     payload) and a parts-waiting indicator.
  3. A/P and Cores/Returns are ~20 lines of fixture from the same jsdom
     coverage now that harness.mjs exists.
  4. The Reports chart is the only chart in the app; the dashboard/board
     could use the same barChart() for cost-by-status or aging buckets.
  5. Debounced input on the estimate's money/qty fields (carried over).
  6. Tab out of the last estimate row should add a new line (carried over).
  7. Persist the message log to localStorage, capped (carried over).
